.TH CVEC_RESET 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cvec_reset \- clear all elements and shrink back to minimum capacity (type-inferred)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cvector.h>

cvec_reset(v);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro is a wrapper for cvector_reset(). It removes every element. It
then tries to shrink the backing buffer back to the minimum capacity of 4
elements. The element count becomes 0, whether the shrink succeeds or
fails. A failed reallocation leaves the larger buffer in place. That is not
an error, and the macro does not report it.

The vector stays usable after this call, and it keeps its element type. You
therefore need no new declaration before you push into it again.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
v    Vector to reset
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The element count becomes 0.
The capacity goes back to the minimum of 4 when the reallocation
succeeds. It stays the same in every other case.
The macro does not free the elements one by one when the vector holds
pointers to heap buffers. The caller must free the element data first
when this is necessary.
This call invalidates every pointer from an earlier cvec_data_ptr(3) or
cvec_at_ptr(3).
The macro asserts when v is NULL.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cvec_construct(vec, int);
cvec_push(vec, 1);
cvec_push(vec, 2);
cvec_reset(vec);
// cvec_size(vec) == 0, vec is still a usable int vector
cvec_destroy(vec);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cvector_reset (3),
.BR cvector_destroy (3),
.BR cvec_size (3)
